Title: **Midnight In Paris**
Credit: Written by
Author: Woody Allen

= While on a trip to Paris with his fiancée's family, a nostalgic screenwriter finds himself mysteriously going back to the 1920s every day at midnight.

# ACT ONE

## SEQUENCE ONE

= An opening montage shows Paris in daylight, then rain, then dusk. During the credits, Gil and Inez discuss the city; Gil loves it and fantasizes about living there in the 1920s, while Inez prefers the United States.

.OPENING MONTAGE [[3.25 pages]] 

Daylight. Shots of Parisian landmarks and people set the tone. Warm, lively, charming.

It begins to drizzle. Umbrellas pop open and people in coats run for cover as the rain thickens. On the roads, the occasional car with the windshield wipers on. Empty tables outside cafes and restaurants.

Then the rain stops, making way for Paris at dusk. The glow of streetlights makes the city all the more romantic and magical.

We see the Eiffel tower. Bright against the night, it's beautiful.

.CREDIT SEQUENCE [[0.75 pages]]

White titles on black. Voiceover of Gil and Inez. Gil gushes about Paris.

GIL 
I mean, this is unbelievable. Look at this. There’s no city like this in the world. There never was!

INEZ
You act like you’ve never been here before.

GIL
I don’t get here often enough. That’s the problem. Can you picture how drop-dead gorgeous this city is in the rain? Imagine this town in the twenties. Paris in the twenties. In the rain. The artists and writers.

## SEQUENCE TWO

= At Monet's Gardens, Gil suggests moving to Paris but Inez dismisses it as fantasy. At dinner with her parents, Gil clashes politically. They encounter friends Paul and Carol Bates and agree to visit Versailles. In their hotel room, Inez criticizes Gil for being anti-social. At Versailles, Paul lectures pompously and Inez reveals Gil's novel struggles. Inez and her mother discuss wedding plans and Gil's Paris fantasies. At the Rodin Museum, Paul argues with the guide. Paul invites them dancing, but Gil declines to get air while Inez goes.

.MONET'S GARDENS [[0.5 pages]]

Gil expresses love for Paris and desire to move there. Inez dismisses it as fantasy and refuses to live outside the US.

GIL (V. O.)
I mean, look, this is where Monet lived and painted. We’re thirty minutes from town. Imagine the two of us settling here. We could do it. I mean, if my book turns out...

INEZ
You’re in love with a fantasy.

GIL
I’m in love with you.

They kiss but Inez pulls away to check her watch.

.LE GRAND VEFOUR RESTAURANT [[2.75 pages]]

Dinner with his in-laws John and Helen. It's obvious they don't share Gil's enthusiasm for Paris. They're not on the same page on business and politics either.

INEZ
Oh please, let's not get into that discussion yet again.

GIL
By the way, it's fine for your father and I to disagree. That's what a democracy is. Your father defends the right wing of the Republican party and I happen to think you've almost got to be like a demented lunatic. But it doesn't mean we don't respect each other's views. Am I right?

By coincidence, Inez's friends from the States PAUL AND CAROL BATES as her mother brings up wedding plans. They're in France because Paul's going to lecture at The Sorbonne.

Despite Gil's obvious reluctance, they get roped into a trip to Versailles.

.HOTEL ROOM [[1.25 pages]]

Once in the privacy of their suite, Inez expresses her displeasure at Gil being *anti-social* with Paul and Carol earlier at dinner. Gil admits they're not his crowd.

INEZ
He's brilliant. You know, I had such a crush on him in college. And Carol's very bright.

GIL
I mean he's a pseudo-intellectual. Just a little bit.

INEZ
Uhhh, Gil? I hardly think he'd be lecturing at ***THE SORBONNE*** if he's a pseudo-intellectual!

.VERSAILLES TRIP [[2.5 pages]]

Paul is insufferable as their self-declared tour guide. Inez publicly reveals Gil's struggles with his novel. 

PAUL
I believe Louis moved his court here in 1682. Originally all this was swampland. In fact, if I'm not mistaken, in old French the word Versailles means something like "terrain where the weeds have been pulled"...

Inez eventually brings up Gil's book qualms. She reveals that Gil's main character works in a nostalgia shop.

.CHOPARD STORE DISPLAY [[0.5 pages]]

Inez and her mother gush about her upcoming wedding, especially the diamond ring. Her mother voices doubts about Gil's yearning for Paris. Inez assures her it's just talk.

HELEN
Well, that frightens me.

.RODIN MUSEUM GARDEN [[1.25 pages]]

A female guide shows the two couples Rodin's most famous statue. Paul insists Rodin's wife was Camille and that Rodin was actually married to Rose.

CAROL
Are you arguing with the guide?

PAUL
Yes, I am.

.ROOFTOP WINE TASTING / STREET OUTSIDE [[2.5 pages]]

Paul and Carol invite them dancing.

GIL
I don't want to be a killjoy but I need to get a little fresh air.

Inez chooses to go dancing.

## SEQUENCE THREE

= Gil gets lost walking through Paris. He finds a set of stairs and sits down. A clock rings midnight and a vintage 1928 Peugeot pulls up full of party goers. They convince Gil to come along. Gil attends a 1920s party where he meets Scott and Zelda Fitzgerald, then Ernest Hemingway. After discussing writing, Gil retrieves his manuscript but finds the cafe has been replaced by a laundry, returning him to the present.

.LOST [[3 pages]]

Gil wanders the streets until he's lost. Tired, he sits on some steps just as a bell rings. It's midnight.

A vintage Peugeot rolls up the street, stopping in front of him. A man steps out and ushers him in. Disoriented, Gil goes with it.

Inside the car, he's sandwiched between women dressed in the style of the twenties. They pour him champagne. Gil drinks up. They hop off at an old building.

.THE FITZGERALD'S PARTY [[3.75 pages]]

Gil arrives at some sort of costume party. The man on piano singing *Let's Fall In Love* is a dead ringer for Cole Porter. Gil questions his reality.

A woman with a pronounced Southern accent zones in on Gil. Gil introduces himself as a writer. At that, the woman gives her name—Zelda—and calls for a tall lean. He comes over and shakes Gil's hand, says he's Scott Fitzgerald.

SCOTT
And who are you, old sport?

GIL
Gil—you have the same names as the...

SCOTT
As what?

GIL
(Gestruting)
Scott Fitzgerald and...

SCOTT
Zelda Fitzgerald. The Fitzgeralds! Isn't she beautiful?

Scott pulls Zelda into him and they smile winsomely.

.BRICKTOP'S [[1 pages]]

Gil and the Fitzgeralds watch a woman dancing. She resembles Josephine Baker. Gil sits in stunned silence. In time he breaks into a smile.

.LE POLIDOR [[5.25 pages]]

Gil and the Fitzgeralds jump to another cafe where they introduce him to Ernest Hemingway. Gil is starstruck.

ZELDA
(To Hemingway)
Did you read my story? What'd you think?

HEMINGWAY
There was some fine writing in it, but it was unfulfilled.

Zelda is offended and since Scott doesn't defend her, she latches on to a passing man. It's Juan Belmonte.

ZELDA 
If you're gonna stay here and drink with him, I'm going with the toreador.

Hemingway advises Gil on writing and refuses to read his novel, instead suggesting they take it to Gertrude Stein. 

Gil goes to get his manuscript.

He returns to Le Polidor only to find a laundry shop in its place.

He's back in the present.

# ACT TWO (A)

## SEQUENCE FOUR

= In a hotel room, Gil tells Inez about meeting the Fitzgeralds and Hemingway, but she dismisses him. At a furniture store, Helen pressures them to buy an expensive chair. Later, Gil returns to the steps and is picked up by Hemingway. In the car, they discuss fear of death. At Gertrude Stein's, Gil meets Adriana and they bond over their shared love for the past.

.HOTEL ROOM [[1.25 pages]]

The next morning, Gil tells Inez how he met the Fitzgeralds and Hemingway. 

Inez glosses over this, busy getting dressed. They have plans but Gil wants to stay in. Work on his novel.

INEZ
No, you can do that later. Mom said we could use her decorator's discount. Get up.

.FURNITURE STORE [[1.25 pages]]

Helen talks up an 18,000-euro chair to Inez and Gil.

GIL
But we haven't even found a house yet and we're trying to keep expenses down so I don't have to take any crummy rewrite jobs.

HELEN
(not impressed)
Well, you get what you pay for. Cheap is cheap.

They exit the shop. It's raining and Gil invites them to walk.

Huddled under an umbrella, Helen and Inez head for a car.

INEZ
Gil, no. There's nothing beautiful about walking in the rain.

HELEN
Don't forget we're taking you to dinner tonight.

.THE STEPS [[2.5 pages]]

With his manuscript and Inez in tow, Gil returns to the steps where the car picked him up.

Time passes... and nothing. Inez hails a taxi.

INEZ
If I'm asleep when you get in don't wake me up.

Alone, Gil wonders what he's doing wrong. As he recounts the previous night, the clock strikes midnight.

An old car arrives. Gil hops in, greeting Hemingway.

.PERIOD CAR [[1.75 pages]]

Hemingway shares his war story. Gil asks if he was ever scared of getting killed.

HEMINGWAY
You'll never write well if you fear dying. Do you?

GIL
Yeah, I do. I'd say it's probably my greatest fear actually.

.GERTRUDE STEIN'S [[6.25 pages]]

Hemingway introduces Gil to Gertrude Stein as she's critiquing Pablo Picasso's painting of his lover, Adriana. Stein argues he's over-sexualized it. It doesn't capture Adriana's true allure.

STEIN (TO GIL)
What is your first impression of Adriana?

Gil looks to the woman leaning casually against the doorframe.

GIL 
Exceptionally lovely.

With Gil's novel in her hands, Stein reads the opening lines out loud to the room.

ADRIANA
I love it. I'm already hooked.

Stein calls Hemingway over to talk business, allowing Gil to approach Adriana.

GIL
Did my opening lines really get to you that strongly?

ADRIANA
The past has always had a great charisma for me.

They discover a shared love for the past, only the time Adriana longs for was *La Belle Époque Paris*.

## SEQUENCE FIVE

= At night Gil lies in bed while Inez sleeps. He's buzzing, murmuring to himself about  Gertrude Stein, Hemingway and everyone he's met from the past. At a museum with Inez, Paul, and Carol, he challenges Paul's art lecture with his newfound knowledge of the past. Later, Gil attends a Fitzgerald party where he dances with Adriana and rescues Zelda from the river. At a cafe, Adriana discovers Gil is engaged and leaves upset. Gil then explains his time-traveling situation to Dali, Buñuel, and Man Ray, confessing his love for Adriana.

.HOTEL ROOM [[0.75 pages]]

Back in the present, in bed with a sleeping Inez, Gil's still awake whispering to himself.

GIL
I'm Gil Pender from Pasadena. Cub Scouts. I failed freshman English. But little 'ol Gil Pender has his novel with Gertrude Stein. Oh boy, that girl was so... lovely.

.SAINT-OUEN FLEA MARKET [[2 pages]]

Separating from Inez and Helen, Gil follows a Cole Porter song to a record store. The pretty shop-worker asks if he likes Cole Porter.

GIL
I do, I'm a big fan. Yeah, I like to... I like to think that I'm a member of Linda and Cole's inner circle... I'm kidding.

They share a friendly moment before Inez sweeps in, reminds Gil of another museum date with Paul and Carol.

.MUSEUM [[1.75 pages]]

Gil, Inez, Paul, and Carol admire a Monet painting. Paul dominates the conversation. Gil wants to contribute.

INEZ
Shhh! I'm trying to hear Paul say.

On another floor, they stop at Picasso's painting of Adriana. Paul starts another mini-lecture about it.

GIL
Uhh Paul—I'm going to have to differ with you on this one.  If I’m not mistaken this was a failed attempt to capture a young French girl named Adriana—from Bordeaux, if my art history serves me—who came to Paris to study costume design for the theatre. 

INEZ
What have you been smoking?

.RESTAURANT [[1.25 pages]]

Inez is finishing up dinner with her parents. Gil has gone on another walk.

INEZ
The way the city's lit up at night allegedly inspires him. So... But that's okay because I'm going dancing with Paul.

Inez leaves her parents to discuss Gil's strangeness and whereabouts. Helen doesn't support John's idea of having Gil followed.

JOHN
No? I'd like to go where he goes every night.

.FITZGERALD PARTY [[3.25 pages]]

Gil's dancing at a carnival-themed party. He runs into Adriana who leads him to a carousel.

ADRIANA
It's from the turn of the century, isn't it beautiful? It's my favorite era.

Hemingway, with Belmonte in tow, stumbles in drunk and flirts with Adriana. Gil offers to take Adriana somewhere quieter.

Outside, Adriana confides in Gil that she's broken up with Picasso.

.PARIS WALK [[1.75 pages]]

Gil and Adriana take an idyllic stroll. They reach a street lined with sex workers.

ADRIANA
See anything you like?

GIL
Yeah, I'm ashamed to admit I'm attracted to ALL of them.

ADRIANA
When I was in Catholic school, one weekend, my roommate and I, we paid a girl from Pigalle to come and teach us all her tricks.

GIL
Wow... That's... I'm gonna be thinking about that one.

.RIVERSIDE [[1 pages]]

Gil and Adriana stop Zelda from jumping into the river. Zelda thinks Scott is unfaithful.

ZELDA
The more they drank the more he fell in love with her!

Zelda tries to jump again but Adriana catches her. Gil gives Zelda a Valium.

ADRIANA
You carry medicine?

GIL
No, not normally. Just since I've been engaged to Inez I've been having, just little panic attacks but I'm sure they'll subside after the wedding.

ZELDA
I've never heard of Valium. What is this?

GIL
The pill of the future.

.CAFE [[1.25 pages]]

ADRIANA
You never said you were getting married.

GIL
Yeah, yeah. I'm uh... You know, it’s still a ways in the future.

ADRIANA
Well, good luck with your book and your wedding.

Adriana leaves, gently turning down Gil's offer to escort her home.

.CAFE BOOTH [[3.5 pages]]

Gil meets Salvador Dali, Luis Buñuel, and Man Ray and shares his 'perplexing situation'.

GIL
I’m from a different time—another era, the future. Okay? I come from the two-thousandth millennium to here. I get in a car and I slide through time.

MAN RAY
Exactly correct. You inhabit two worlds. So far I see nothing strange.

GIL
Well yeah, you’re surrealists. But I’m a normal guy. See, in one life I’m engaged to marry a woman I love... at least I think I love her. Christ, I better love her, I’m marrying her.

Gil confesses about Adriana.

MAN RAY
A man in love with a woman from a different era...

# ACT TWO (B)

## SEQUENCE SIX

= Inez declines Gil's advances in their hotel room, telling him about Paul's lunch invitation. Gil asks a museum guide about loving two women. John hires a detective to follow Gil. Gertrude Stein informs Gil that Adriana's relationships with Picasso and Hemingway have ended. Gil buys earrings and discovers Adriana's journal expressing her love for him. He steals Inez's earrings but is caught when she returns early; he finds the missing earrings. Gil buys new earrings and is followed by the detective. He visits Stein, learns Adriana is at a wedding, and goes to see her. They kiss, and Gil gives her the earrings before a carriage takes them to the Belle Epoque.

.HOTEL ROOM [[0.75 pages]]

Inez eats room service in a towel. Gil wants to get it on but Inez swats him off. She says Paul has invited them for lunch in the countryside.

INEZ
But if you don't want to go... Don't go. I'm not missing it because of you.

.RODIN MUSEUM GARDEN [[1 pages]]

Gil revisits the guide to ask about Rodin's wife and mistress.

GIL
Do you think that's possible to love two women at once?

GUIDE
Well, he loved them both but in a different way.

.DULUC DETECTIVE [[1.5 pages]]

John meets with Dubois to have Gil followed. Dubois assigns Tisserant to the case.

At midnight Tisserant watches from the shadows as Gil gets into a car with T.S. Elliot.

.GERTRUDE STEIN'S [[1 pages]]

Stein reveals Adriana left Picasso and has flown to Africa with Hemingway. Stein then gives feedback on Gil's book.

STEIN
The artist’s job is not to succumb to despair but to find an antidote for the emptiness of existence. You have a clear and lively voice— don’t be such a defeatist.

.OUSIDE HOTEL / FLEA MARKET [[3.5 pages]]

Inez and her parents load up a car for a weekend trip. Inez informs them Gil won't be joining them because he's writing.

Meanwhile, Gil returns to the flea market. He buys a Cole Porter album from Gabrielle, the shop-keeper girl and an old journal from a bookseller.

EXT. NOTRE DAME

At Notre Dame, Gil listens to the museum guide as she translates what sound like journal entries.

GUIDE
I’m in love with an American writer I just met named Gil Pender.

It's Adriana's journal.

GUIDE
I know that both Picasso and Hemingway are in love with me but for whatever inexplicable reasons the heart has, I am drawn by Gil... As always in this sad life, he is about to marry a woman named Inez. I had a dream where he came to me and brought me a little gift, earrings they were— and we made love.

.HOTEL SUITE [[4.5 pages]]

Gil steals a pair of Inez's earrings and wraps them in a small box.

On his way out, Inez and her parents return. Turns out John's having chest pain. Helen calls for the hotel doctor.

Inez asks why Gil is dressed up. Gil says he's been writing.

INEZ
You dress up and put on cologne to write?

GIL
No, I took a— I jumped in the shower for a second because you know how I think better in the shower and get the positive ions going in there...

Inez spots the box in Gil's hand. She asks if it's a present for her. Gil goes with it, saying it's from the flea market.

INEZ (DOUBTFUL)
I hope it's my taste if it's jewelry, because that moonstone necklace...

Wanting to prove a point, Inez searches her jewelry and discovers her pearl earrings are gone.

INEZ
Do you think it was the maid?

HELEN
It's always the maid.

They're about to report the theft when the hotel doctor arrives to examine John. 

Gil *finds* the missing earrings.

.STREET VENDOR / DETECTIVE'S CAR [[0.5 pages]]

Gil buys new earrings from a vendor.

In a car the detective Tisserant watches Gil's ride pull away. Tisserant tails them.

INT. GLORIA STEINS [[0.75 pages]]

Stein is speaking with Henri Matisse in her study when Gil drops off his revised manuscript. He asks after Hemingway.

STEIN
Oh, they've been back for days now. The trip didn't work out. I knew they wouldn't hit it off. It's over. With Picasso, too.

Stein says Adriana's alone, attending a wedding at Deyrolle's.

STEIN
She'll be glad to see you.

.WEDDING [[2 pages]]

At a wedding party, Gil approaches Adriana. He's straightforward.

GIL
I sense that there's some complicated feelings, maybe—what you have for me.

ADRIANA
Aren't you going to get married?

GIL
Everything's a little up in the air right now. I don't know what is gonna happen.

Gil invites her out to talk more privately. They leave together.

.ROMANTIC STREET [[2.75 pages]]

Passing by streetlamps, Gil kisses Adriana.

ADRIANA
What are you doing?

GIL
I don't know.

They stop to sit outside a cafe.

GIL
But I did feel—for a minute there while I was doing it—like I was... immortal.

ADRIANA
But you look so sad.

He gives Adriana the earrings he got earlier. She says they're *remarkable*.

Before Adriana can put them on, a horse-drawn carriage appears. The passengers call Gil and Inez over.

The carriage takes them even further into the past, to Maxim's during La Belle Epoque.

## SEQUENCE SEVEN

= Gil and Adriana slow dance at Maxim's. At the Moulin Rouge, they meet artists who prefer the Renaissance. Adriana wants to stay in the 1890s, but Gil reveals he's from 2010. They kiss goodbye. At Gertrude Stein's, she and Hemingway critique Gil's novel.

.MAXIM'S [[1.25 pages]]

Gil and Adriana slow dance to live music.

ADRIANA
Isn't it amazing? The first time we met, I told you about this place and the Belle Epoque. And here we are.

.MOULIN ROUGE [[6.25 pages]]

After catching a performance, Gil and Adriana come to sit with Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec.

Paul Gauguin and Edgar Degas join in. The artists believe the renaissance is the true golden age. Gauguin asks Adriana if she's interested in doing costumes for a new ballet. She's thrilled, until...

ADRIANA
Oh my God. I—I don't live here, I mean, I do but I don't but I—

GIL
It's best not to go into too many details. We're temporarily passing through.

Adriana asks to speak with Gil alone. They go off to the side.

ADRIANA
Let's never go back to the twenties.

Gil reminds her of their friends.

ADRIANA
But it's the present. It's dull.

GIL
Dull? It's not MY present. I'm from two thousand and ten... I dropped in on you, the same way we're dropping in on the 1890's... I was trying to escape my present the same way you're trying to escape yours to a Golden Age.

Adriana says goodbye, kissing him for the last time.

.GERTRUDE STEIN'S [[0.75 pages]]

Stein's happy with Gil's rewrite. Hemingway's read it too and Stein relays his plot suggestion for Gil.

STEIN
Well, he doesn't quite believe that the protagonist doesn't see that his fiancee is having an affair right before his eyes.

GIL
With?

STEIN
The other character. The pedantic one.

GIL
That's called denial. Thank you.

# ACT FOUR

## SEQUENCE 8

= Gil confronts Inez about her affair with Paul; she confesses. He announces he's staying in Paris and breaking up with her. Her parents arrive; Gil leaves. John reveals the detective following Gil is missing. Detective Tisserant is seen fleeing from a historical French court after startling its occupants. Gil stops along the Pont Alexandre III and runs into Gabrielle, the record store girl. He tells her he's moving to Paris. They talk, exchange names, and begin walking home together in the rain.

.HOTEL SUITE / FRENCH COURT [[3 pages]]

Gil confronts Inez about her affair with Paul. She initially denies it but then confesses after he mentions Hemingway. 

Gil announces he is staying in Paris and breaking up with her. 

GIL
I'm not going back... I'm staying here and it has nothing to do with, you know, your romantic fling. Paris is Paris. Inez, you and I, it's obvious. I don't think we're right for each other.

Her parents arrive; Gil leaves. John reveals the detective following Gil is missing. 

The detective, Tisserant, is seen fleeing from a historical French court after startling its occupants.

Gil exits. John reveals he had Gil followed and that the detective is reportedly missing.

Cut to Detective Tisserant entering a period room from the royal French court. Shocked, the occupants call guards on him. Tisserant flees.

.MIDNIGHT IN PARIS [[2.75 pages]]

Gil stops along the Pont Alexandre III to admire the Eiffel Tower.

He runs into the shopkeeper girl from the record store. She's on her way home from a late dinner. He shares he's moving to Paris.

GABRIELLE
You know, I thought about you the other day... My boss got a whole new album of Cole Porter songs.

GIL
And that made you think about me? I like to be thought of that way.

Just as he asks if he can walk her home and buy her coffee, it begins to rain. The woman says she doesn't mind the rain.

GABRIELLE
Actually, Paris is the most beautiful in the rain.

They exchange names. She goes by Gabrielle.

GIL
It's a pretty name.

And so, their walk home begins.

.CREDITS [[3.25 pages]]